\title{Generalising the Cauchy Integral Formula}
\date{2023-01-15}
\author{JB00}
\import{macros}

\p{\em{Goal:} generalise the CIF by allowing more general closed curves for the integration. We have an issue: let #{\gamma_1(t) = e^{2\pi it}}, #{t \in [0, 1]}, and #{\gamma_2(t) = e^{4\pi it}}, #{t \in [0, 1]}. Then #{\int_{\gamma_2} f = 2\int_{\gamma_1} f}.}

\p{We need to deal with the issue of “winding around” a point more than once; however, once we quantify this notion, we’ll see it is the \em{only} issue to generalising the CIF.}

\p{Naive hope: “counting” crossings of a slit in the plane — but a curve can cross a slit back and forth and only some crossings matter, so this is awkward to make rigorous.}

